# iOS SDK Isolation — Move Product Features Back to the App

## Context

The `ripul-ios-sdk` (SPM module `RipulAgent`) started as a clean embedding SDK: `AgentView`, `AgentBridge`, `NativeTool`, `CapabilityHandler`. The public API was generic — any app could drop it in, register tools, and have a working AI agen

Over time, product features migrated into the SDK because `AgentBridge` owns the `WKWebView` and adding a `public func` that calls a `__ripul*` JS global is the path of least resistance. Each addition was small and justified, but cumulative

### Current state

| Metric | Value |
|--------|-------|
| `AgentBridge.swift` | 3,435 lines |
| `@Published` properties | ~40 |
| Public API surface | ~271 members |
| SDK total | 9,414 lines across 22 files |
| External consumers | 1 (`ripul-native-app`) |

### What leaked in

1. **Remote machine infrastructure** — `connectToMachine`, `listRemoteSessions`, `killMachine`, `setHostEnabled`, `getHostStatus`, relay discovery/execution, `RemoteSessionInfo` (~650 lines)
2. **CLI account & billing** — `CliUsageStats`, `CliRateLimits`, `CliAccountInfo`, `fetchCliAccount` (~150 lines)
3. **CLI session management** — `importCliSession`, `onCliSessionRenamed`, rename detection baked into `fetchSessions()`, `setCliPlanMode`/`onPlanModeChanged` (~100 lines)
4. **Todo/plan state** — `TodoState`, `TodoItem`, `todoStates`, `dismissedTodoVersions`, `listViewedTodoVersions`, `todoStateSubject`, `TodoLozenge.swift` (~140 lines + full file)

